<div class="component container col-12">
    <div class="component-content" >

    <div class="component footnotes col-12">
        <div class="component-content">
<div class="cf-footnotes">
    <h5>Footnotes</h5>
        <ol>
                    <li id="cf-fn-1"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">See Const&acirc;ncio (2015).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-1" aria-label="Return from footnote 1.">Return to 1</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-2"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">See Del Negro et al. (2020).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-2" aria-label="Return from footnote 2.">Return to 2</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-3"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">The Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland produces the median and trimmed-mean CPI inflation rate and the median PCE inflation rate. The Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas produces the trimmed-mean PCE inflation rate. The Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland&rsquo;s Center for Inflation Research produces inflation measures and analyses of inflation and inflation expectations to inform policymakers, researchers, and the general public.</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-3" aria-label="Return from footnote 3.">Return to 3</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-4"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">The FOMC&rsquo;s statement on longer-run goals and monetary policy strategy, revised in 2020 and reaffirmed since then, says that the Committee judges that longer-term inflation expectations that are well anchored at 2 percent contribute to achieving its monetary policy goals. See Federal Open Market Committee (2022a).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-4" aria-label="Return from footnote 4.">Return to 4</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-5"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">See Phelps (1967), Friedman (1968), and Lucas (1972). Empirical work on the determinants of inflation finds that the output gap matters when it is large and that, in recent years, forward-looking measures of inflation expectations play a larger role in explaining inflation dynamics than do backward-looking measures.</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-5" aria-label="Return from footnote 5.">Return to 5</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-6"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">For further discussion, see Fuhrer and Olivei (2009) and Clark and Davig (2009).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-6" aria-label="Return from footnote 6.">Return to 6</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-7"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">See Trehan and Lynch (2013) and Hattori and Yetman (2017).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-7" aria-label="Return from footnote 7.">Return to 7</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-8"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">The index of common inflation expectations is a research data series maintained by the Board of Governors&rsquo; staff. See Ahn and Fulton (2021).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-8" aria-label="Return from footnote 8.">Return to 8</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-9"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">See Candia, Coibion, and Gorodnichenko (2021).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-9" aria-label="Return from footnote 9.">Return to 9</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-10"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">As discussed in De Pooter, et al. (2016), survey measures of the inflation expectations of professional forecasters and financial industry participants were fairly stable over the course of the Great Recession and recovery, while those of households and businesses drifted down.</span><br style="color: #201f1f; background-color: #ffffff;" />
<span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">In addition, the Cleveland Fed&rsquo;s indirect consumer inflation expectations measure indicates that women, older respondents, and more educated respondents have tended to expect higher inflation over the next year than do their counterparts. The Cleveland Fed&rsquo;s indirect consumer inflation expectations measure, which started in 2021, is based on a nationwide survey with more than 10,000 responses and is updated on a weekly basis. Instead of asking consumers directly about overall inflation, the survey asks consumers how they expect the prices of the things they buy to change over the next 12 months and how much their incomes would have to change for them to be able to afford the same consumption basket and be equally well-off. See Hajdini, et al. (2022).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-10" aria-label="Return from footnote 10.">Return to 10</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-11"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">As discussed in De Pooter, et al. (2016), survey measures of the inflation expectations of professional forecasters and financial industry participants were fairly stable over the course of the Great Recession and recovery, while those of households and businesses drifted down. In addition, the Cleveland Fed&rsquo;s indirect consumer inflation expectations measure indicates that women, older respondents, and more educated respondents have tended to expect higher inflation over the next year than do their counterparts. The Cleveland Fed&rsquo;s indirect consumer inflation expectations measure, which started in 2021, is based on a nationwide survey with more than 10,000 responses and is updated on a weekly basis. Instead of asking consumers directly about overall inflation, the survey asks consumers how they expect the prices of the things they buy to change over the next 12 months and how much their incomes would have to change for them to be able to afford the same consumption basket and be equally well-off. See Hajdini, et al. (2022).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-11" aria-label="Return from footnote 11.">Return to 11</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-12"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">Starting in September, the Fed will allow up to $60 billion per month of Treasury securities and up to $35 billion per month of agency securities to run off the balance sheet. To the extent that maturing Treasury coupon securities are less than the monthly cap, Treasury bills will make up the rest of the runoff up to the cap. See the Federal Open Market Committee (2022b).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-12" aria-label="Return from footnote 12.">Return to 12</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-13"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">See Federal Open Market Committee (2022c).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-13" aria-label="Return from footnote 13.">Return to 13</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-14"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">See Mester (2016). Aoki (2003) studied the optimal policy response when data are measured with error and concluded that the degree of response to a variable in the policy rule should be less the higher the variable&rsquo;s measurement error. Brainard (1967) studied optimal policy in response to a shock when there is uncertainty about the effect of policy on the economy and concluded that policy should respond less when there is uncertainty than when there is no uncertainty. It has been shown that this result is not general across models.</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-14" aria-label="Return from footnote 14.">Return to 14</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-15"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">Giannoni (2002 and 2007) shows policymakers averse to uncertainty will react more strongly to fluctuations in inflation and the output gap than if there were no uncertainty. They would put more weight on stabilizing inflation and the output gap and less weight on stabilizing the nominal interest rate.</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-15" aria-label="Return from footnote 15.">Return to 15</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-16"><span style="background-color: #ffffff; color: #201f1f;">See De Pooter, et al. (2016) and Walsh (2022).</span>
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-16" aria-label="Return from footnote 16.">Return to 16</a>
                    </li>
        </ol>
</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="component notes col-12">
        <div class="component-content">
<h5 class="field-title">References</h5><ul>
    <li class="reference-item" style="margin-bottom: 4px;">Ahn, Hie Joo, and Chad Fulton, &ldquo;Research Data Series: Index of Common Inflation Expectations,&rdquo; FEDS Notes, Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, March 5, 2021. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.17016/2380-7172.2873" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.17016/2380-7172.2873</a>)</li>
    <li>Aoki, Kosuke, &ldquo;On the Optimal Monetary Policy Response to Noisy Indicators,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Journal of Monetary Economics</em>&nbsp;50, April 2003, pp. 501-523. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0304-3932(03)00023-0" target="_blank">http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0304-3932(03)00023-0</a>)</li>
    <li>Brainard, William C., &ldquo;Uncertainty and the Effectiveness of Policy,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>American Economic Review: Papers and Proceedings</em>&nbsp;57, May 1967, pp. 411-425. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/1821642#metadata_info_tab_contents" target="_blank">https://www.jstor.org/stable/1821642#metadata_info_tab_contents</a>)</li>
    <li>Candia, Bernardo, Olivier Coibion, and Yuriy Gorodnichenko, &ldquo;The Inflation Expectations of U.S. Firms: Evidence from a New Survey,&rdquo; National Bureau of Economic Research Working Paper 28836, May 2021. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.nber.org/papers/w28836" target="_blank">http://www.nber.org/papers/w28836</a>)</li>
    <li>Clark, Todd E., and Troy Davig, &ldquo;The Relationship Between Inflation and Inflation Expectations,&rdquo; memo to the FOMC, November 30, 2009, authorized for public release by the FOMC Secretariat on 4/29/2016. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC20091201memo05.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC20091201memo05.pdf</a>)</li>
    <li>Const&acirc;ncio, V&iacute;tor, &ldquo;Understanding Inflation Dynamics and Monetary Policy,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City Jackson Hole Economic Policy Symposium</em>, 2015. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.kansascityfed.org/documents/7077/ConstancioPanelist_JH2015.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.kansascityfed.org/documents/7077/ConstancioPanelist_JH2015.pdf</a>)</li>
    <li>Del Negro, Marco, Michele Lenza, Giorgio E. Primiceri, and Andrea Tambalotti, &ldquo;What&rsquo;s Up with the Phillips Curve?&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Brookings Papers on Economic Activity</em>, Spring, 2020, pp. 301-373.</li>
    <li>De Pooter, Michiel, Alan Detmeister, Eric Engstrom, Don Kim, David Lebow, Canlin Li, Elmar Mertens, Jeremy Nalewaik, Marius Rodriguez, Jae Sim, Brad Strum, Robert Tetlow, Min Wei, and Emre Yoldas, &ldquo;Longer-Term Inflation Expectations: Evidence and Policy Implications,&rdquo; memo to the FOMC, March 4, 2016, authorized for public release by the FOMC Secretariat on 1/14/2022. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC20160304memo07.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC20160304memo07.pdf</a>)</li>
    <li>Federal Open Market Committee, &ldquo;Statement on Longer-Run Goals and Monetary Policy Strategy,&rdquo; reaffirmed effective January 25, 2022a. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC_LongerRunGoals.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC_LongerRunGoals.pdf</a>)</li>
    <li>Federal Open Market Committee, &ldquo;Plans for Reducing the Size of the Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Balance Sheet,&rdquo; May 4, 2022b. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220504b.htm" target="_blank">https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220504b.htm</a>)</li>
    <li>Federal Open Market Committee, &ldquo;Summary of Economic Projections,&rdquo; September 21, 2022c. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcprojtabl20220921.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcprojtabl20220921.pdf</a>)</li>
    <li>Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland&rsquo;s Center for Inflation Research. (<a href="/center-for-inflation-research">https://www.clevelandfed.org/en/our-research/center-for-inflation-research.aspx</a>)</li>
    <li>Friedman, Milton, &ldquo;The Role of Monetary Policy,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>American Economic Review</em>&nbsp;58, 1968, pp. 1-17. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/1831652" target="_blank">https://www.jstor.org/stable/1831652</a>)</li>
    <li>Fuhrer, Jeff, and Giovanni Olivei, &ldquo;The Role of Expectations and Output in the Inflation Process: An Empirical Assessment,&rdquo; memo to the FOMC, November 30, 2009, authorized for public release by the FOMC Secretariat on 4/29/2016. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC20091201memo04.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC20091201memo04.pdf</a>)</li>
    <li>Giannoni, Marc P., &ldquo;Does Model Uncertainty Justify Caution? Robust Optimal Monetary Policy in a Forward-Looking Model,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Macroeconomic Dynamics</em>&nbsp;6, 2002, pp. 111-144. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S1365100502027062" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.1017/S1365100502027062</a>)</li>
    <li>Giannoni, Marc P., &ldquo;Robust Optimal Monetary Policy in a Forward-Looking Model with Parameter and Shock Uncertainty,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Journal of Applied Econometrics</em>&nbsp;22, 2007, pp. 179-213. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.1002/jae.934" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.1002/jae.934</a>)</li>
    <li>Hajdini, Ina, Edward S. Knotek II, Mathieu Pedemonte, Robert Rich, John Leer, and Raphael Schoenle, &ldquo;Indirect Consumer Inflation Expectations,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Economic Commentary</em>, Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, Number 2022-03, March 1, 2022. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-ec-202203" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-ec-202203</a>)</li>
    <li>Hattori, Masazumi, and James Yetman, &ldquo;The Evolution of Inflation Expectations in Japan,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Journal of the Japanese and International Economies</em>&nbsp;46, 2017, pp. 53-68. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jjie.2017.09.001" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jjie.2017.09.001</a>)</li>
    <li>Lucas, Robert E., Jr., &ldquo;Expectations and the Neutrality of Money,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Journal of Economic Theory 4</em>, 1972, pp. 103-124. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(72)90142-1" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(72)90142-1</a>)</li>
    <li>Mester, Loretta J., &ldquo;Acknowledging Uncertainty,&rdquo; remarks at the Shadow Open Market Committee Fall Meeting, October 7, 2016. (<a href="/collections/speeches/2016/sp-20161007-acknowledging-uncertainty">https://www.clevelandfed.org/newsroom-and-events/speeches/sp-20161007-acknowledging-uncertainty</a>)</li>
    <li>Naggert, Kristoph, Robert Rich, and Joseph Tracy, &ldquo;Flexible Average Inflation Targeting and Inflation Expectations: A Look at the Reaction by Professional Forecasters,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Economic Commentary</em>, Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, Number 2021-09, April 6, 2021. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-ec-202109" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.26509/frbc-ec-202109</a>)</li>
    <li>Phelps, Edmund S., &ldquo;Phillips Curves, Expectations of Inflation, and Optimal Unemployment Over Time,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Economica</em>, 34, 1967, pp. 254-281. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://doi.org/10.2307/2552025" target="_blank">https://doi.org/10.2307/2552025</a>)</li>
    <li>Reis, Ricardo, &ldquo;Losing the Inflation Anchor,&rdquo; Brookings Papers on Economic Activity, BPEA Conference Drafts, September 9, 2021. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.brookings.edu/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/Losing-the-Inflation-Anchor_Conf-Draft.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.brookings.edu/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/Losing-the-Inflation-Anchor_Conf-Draft.pdf</a>)</li>
    <li>Sargent, Thomas J., &ldquo;Comment on &lsquo;Policy Rules for Open Economies,&rsquo; by Laurence Ball,&rdquo; in&nbsp;<em>Monetary Policy Rules</em>, National Bureau of Economic Research Studies in Business Cycles, vol. 31, ed. John B. Taylor, Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1999, pp. 144-154. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c7415/c7415.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.nber.org/system/files/chapters/c7415/c7415.pdf</a>)</li>
    <li>Trehan, Bharat, and Maura Lynch, &ldquo;Consumer Inflation Views in Three Countries,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Economic Letter</em>, Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco, 2013-35, November 25, 2013. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="http://www.frbsf.org/economic-research/publications/economic-letter/2013/november/consumer-inflation-expectations-us-uk-japan-oil-prices/" target="_blank">http://www.frbsf.org/economic-research/publications/economic-letter/2013/november/consumer-inflation-expectations-us-uk-japan-oil-prices/</a>)</li>
    <li>Walsh, Carl E., &ldquo;Implications of a Changing Economic Structure for the Strategy of Monetary Policy,&rdquo;&nbsp;<em>Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City Jackson Hole Economic Policy Symposium</em>, 2003, pp. 297-348. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.kansascityfed.org/Jackson%20Hole/documents/3439/pdf-Walsh2003.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.kansascityfed.org/Jackson%20Hole/documents/3439/pdf-Walsh2003.pdf</a>)</li>
    <li>Walsh, Carl E., &ldquo;Inflation Surges and Monetary Policy,&rdquo; Institute for Monetary and Economic Studies, Bank of Japan, IMES Discussion Paper No. 2022-E-12, July 2022. (<a rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.imes.boj.or.jp/research/papers/english/22-E-12.pdf" target="_blank">https://www.imes.boj.or.jp/research/papers/english/22-E-12.pdf</a>)</li>
